 Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+### 2Q Eviction Strategy
+
+The preview worker uses a 2Q (Two-Queue) replacement policy to balance responsiveness to single screenings with protection for comparison sheets during editing workflows. Entries proceed through two independent FIFO queues:
+
+**Cold Queue (recent first-time accesses)**: A new cached entry starts here. The cold queue receives entries in FIFO order and holds them until:
+- They are accessed a second time (transition to hot), or
+- Their residence time in the cold queue expires (evicted to free space for new entries)
+
+**Hot Queue (reused entries)**: When an entry in the cold queue is accessed again, it moves to the hot queue. The hot queue is FIFO, so reused sheets are protected during long screenings unless the cache must shrink. Within each queue, eviction follows FIFO order: oldest-in-cold evicts before any hot entry, and oldest-in-hot evicts before younger hot entries.
+
+**Queue sizing**: The cache is divided between cold and hot allocations via a tuning parameter (initially 80% hot, 20% cold). This split limits how much a single long screening can fill the cache with one-time sheets. If a queue exceeds its allocation, its oldest entry is evicted to make space; if the other queue is below its allocation, space is freed first from the over-quota queue.
+
+**Admission**: When a completed sheet arrives and the cache is at or above 12 GiB:
+1. If the entry is new (first render), evict from the cold queue until space is available in its allocation
+2. If the entry is a re-render of an existing cold entry (cache hit but re-render due to upgrade), promote to hot and evict from hot if needed
+3. Record the admission to 12 GiB compliance before returning the cached JPEG
+
+Eviction never prevents deduplication: multiple in-flight requests for the same key remain deduped even if the key is not yet cached.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache eviction policy | 2Q (two-queue) | LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging | 2Q protects reused sheets from one-off screening scans by separating first-use entries (cold queue) from reused entries (hot queue). LRU is simpler but a long sequential screening evicts useful comparison sheets before editors can switch back to comparison mode. Frequency-based eviction with aging better retains popular clips but adds counter maintenance overhead and can keep yesterday's favorites around too long when projects change. 2Q's extra bookkeeping (two queues and a tuning parameter) is acceptable to solve the core vulnerability in LRU during editing workflow transitions. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Deferred
+1. **Cold/hot queue size ratio**: Initially 80% hot, 20% cold. This will be tuned based on pilot usage patterns, measured by cache hit rates and eviction rates during comparison vs. screening sessions.
+2. **Cold queue residence time**: When to age out a cold entry without reuse (deferred to implementation; may be unlimited initially, set to a threshold if pilot data shows cold queue fill issues).
